<%# Crawl Run Detail — shows full log for one crawl %>
<%# This is a full page rendered by CrawlsController#show — visited when
    the user clicks "Log →" on a crawl history row (see
    app/views/dashboard/_crawl_history.html.erb). The controller has
    already loaded the specific crawl record into `@crawl_run` and its
    associated log lines into `@log_entries`. %>

<%# "flex items-center justify-between" lays the heading block (left) and
    the "Back to Dashboard" button (right) out horizontally, vertically
    centered, with space pushed between them; "mb-6" adds margin below the
    row before the summary card underneath it. %>
<div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-6">
  <div>
    <%# "text-2xl font-bold text-emerald-400" is a large, bold,
        green-accented heading. `@crawl_run.id` is this record's database
        primary key (an integer, auto-assigned when the row was created),
        printed here as a simple reference number for the crawl. %>
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-emerald-400">Crawl #<%= @crawl_run.id %></h1>
    <%# "text-sm text-gray-400 mt-1" is small, muted subtitle text. %>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-1">
      <%# Prints the city/ZIP that was searched and the radius (in miles)
          that was used for this crawl — both plain saved column values. %>
      <%= @crawl_run.search_city %> — <%= @crawl_run.search_radius_miles %> mile radius
      <%# "&middot;" would be the named HTML entity for a bullet character,
          but this uses "&nbsp;·&nbsp;" instead: "&nbsp;" is the HTML
          entity for a non-breaking space (a space character the browser
          will never wrap a line break onto), and "·" here is the literal
          middle-dot character typed directly into the file — together they
          render as a bullet point visually separated from the text on
          both sides. `.strftime(...)` formats the crawl's creation
          timestamp into text like "July 18, 2026 at 03:45 PM" (%B = full
          month name, %d = day of month, %Y = 4-digit year, %I = 12-hour
          clock hour, %M = minutes, %p = AM/PM). %>
      · <%= @crawl_run.created_at.strftime("%B %d, %Y at %I:%M %p") %>
    </p>
  </div>
  <%# `link_to "← Back to Dashboard", root_path, ...` generates a plain `<a
      href="...">` link. `root_path` is the URL helper for this app's home
      page (the dashboard — see config/routes.rb for the `root` route
      declaration that makes DashboardController#index the site's front
      page). "btn-secondary" is this app's custom CSS class for a
      lower-emphasis button style. %>
  <%= link_to "← Back to Dashboard", root_path, class: "btn-secondary" %>
</div>

<%# Summary card %>
<%# "card" is this app's boxed/panel styling; "mb-6" adds margin below it,
    separating it from the log section further down. %>
<div class="card mb-6">
  <%# "grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6" lays out four stat blocks in a
      grid: 2 columns by default (on narrow/mobile screens), switching to 4
      columns on medium-and-wider screens (the "md:" prefix is a Tailwind
      "responsive variant" — it only applies at that breakpoint and up),
      with consistent spacing (gap-6) between every cell either way. %>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6">
    <div>
      <%# "text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase" is small, muted, all-caps label
          text, used consistently as the caption above each stat value
          below. %>
      <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase">Status</div>
      <%# This <div>'s CSS class is itself computed with a Ruby expression,
          spread across two lines for readability inside the class
          attribute. `@crawl_run.status` is a string column ("completed",
          "failed", or something else e.g. "running"). This is a CHAINED
          (nested) ternary: `a ? b : c ? d : e` reads as "if a, use b;
          otherwise, if c, use d; otherwise use e." Here: if status is
          "completed" use green text; otherwise if status is "failed" use
          red text; otherwise (any other status) use yellow text. "mt-1
          font-semibold" (static, not conditional) adds top margin and
          semi-bold weight regardless of status. %>
      <div class="mt-1 font-semibold
        <%= @crawl_run.status == "completed" ? "text-emerald-400" : @crawl_run.status == "failed" ? "text-red-400" : "text-yellow-400" %>">
        <%# `.capitalize` is a Ruby String method that uppercases just the
            first letter and lowercases the rest — turns "completed" into
            "Completed" for display. %>
        <%= @crawl_run.status.capitalize %>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase">Duration</div>
      <%# `duration_label` is a method defined on the CrawlRun model (not
          shown in this file) that presumably formats the elapsed time into
          a friendly string (e.g. "4m 12s"). %>
      <div class="mt-1 text-white"><%= @crawl_run.duration_label %></div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase">Facilities Found</div>
      <%# Plain integer column, printed as-is. %>
      <div class="mt-1 text-white"><%= @crawl_run.facilities_found %></div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase">Units Found</div>
      <div class="mt-1 text-white"><%= @crawl_run.units_found %></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# `.present?` is true only if `error_message` is both non-nil and
      non-empty — i.e. this box only appears when the crawl actually
      recorded an error. %>
  <% if @crawl_run.error_message.present? %>
    <%# "mt-4 p-3" adds top margin and inner padding; "bg-red-900/20
        border border-red-800/40 rounded" gives it a subtle red-tinted
        alert box appearance; "text-sm text-red-300" is small red text. %>
    <div class="mt-4 p-3 bg-red-900/20 border border-red-800/40 rounded text-sm text-red-300">
      <strong>Error:</strong> <%= @crawl_run.error_message %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
  <%# Closes the `if @crawl_run.error_message.present?` check above. %>
</div>

<%# Log entries %>
<div class="card">
  <%# "text-sm font-semibold text-gray-300 uppercase tracking-wider mb-4"
      styles this as a small, bold, all-caps section heading with letter
      spacing and margin below it. `@log_entries.count` runs a database
      COUNT query (or counts the already-loaded array, depending on how
      `@log_entries` was built in the controller) to show how many log
      lines this crawl produced. %>
  <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-300 uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">
    Full Log (<%= @log_entries.count %> entries)
  </h2>

  <%# "bg-gray-950" is a near-black background, evoking a terminal/console
      look; "rounded border border-gray-800 p-4" gives it a bordered,
      padded box; "font-mono text-xs" uses a monospaced (fixed-width) font
      at small size, typical for log output; "overflow-x-auto" adds a
      horizontal scrollbar if any single log line is too wide to fit;
      "max-h-screen overflow-y-auto" caps this box's height to at most the
      browser window's height and adds a vertical scrollbar for anything
      taller than that, so a huge log doesn't push the rest of the page
      far below the fold. %>
  <div class="bg-gray-950 rounded border border-gray-800 p-4 font-mono text-xs overflow-x-auto max-h-screen overflow-y-auto">
    <%# `@log_entries.each do |entry| ... end` loops over every log entry
        record, running this block once per entry with `entry` bound to
        the current one each time — this is what prints one line per log
        message. %>
    <% @log_entries.each do |entry| %>
      <%# "py-0.5" adds a small amount of vertical padding per line so
          lines aren't crammed together. `entry.css_class` is a method
          defined on the log entry model that presumably returns a
          different text color class depending on the entry's severity
          (e.g. red for errors, yellow for warnings) — its exact value is
          appended into this <div>'s class attribute via `<%= % >`. %>
      <div class="py-0.5 <%= entry.css_class %>">
        <%# Square brackets here are literal, static text (not Ruby/ERB
            syntax) — they visually box in each metadata field the way log
            files conventionally do. `.strftime("%H:%M:%S")` formats the
            entry's timestamp as 24-hour hours:minutes:seconds, e.g.
            "14:03:27". %>
        [<%= entry.created_at.strftime("%H:%M:%S") %>]
        <%# `.upcase` is a Ruby String method converting all letters to
            uppercase — turns a saved level like "warning" into "WARNING". %>
        [<%= entry.level.upcase %>]
        <%# Plain saved column — which company this log line pertains to. %>
        [<%= entry.company %>]
        <%# The actual log message text. %>
        <%= entry.message %>
        <%# `.present?` is true only if this entry recorded a URL — not
            every log line has one (e.g. a generic status message might
            not reference a specific page). %>
        <% if entry.url.present? %>
          <%# "text-gray-600" is dim gray, de-emphasizing the URL relative
              to the main message text; the literal " → " arrow is static
              text pointing from the message to the URL. `.truncate(80)` is
              a Rails/ActiveSupport String helper that cuts the string down
              to at most 80 characters, appending "..." if it had to cut
              anything off — this keeps a very long URL from blowing out
              the line width. %>
          <span class="text-gray-600"> → <%= entry.url.truncate(80) %></span>
        <% end %>
        <%# Closes the `if entry.url.present?` check above. %>
      </div>
    <%# Closes the `@log_entries.each do |entry|` loop opened above. %>
    <% end %>
    <%# `.empty?` is true only when there are zero log entries at all —
        shown as a fallback message instead of an empty blank box. %>
    <% if @log_entries.empty? %>
      <div class="text-gray-600">No log entries found for this crawl run.</div>
    <% end %>
    <%# Closes the `if @log_entries.empty?` check above. %>
  </div>
</div>
